{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc | sed -n '714,835p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc | sed -n '330,470p'\"","aggregated_output":"   714\t(defn- vm-load-program\n   715\t  \"Load one datom transaction into the VM.\"\n   716\t  [^ASTWalkerVM vm datoms]\n   717\t  (let [ast (vm/datoms->ast datoms)]\n   718\t    (assoc vm\n   719\t           :program ast\n   720\t           :control ast\n   721\t           :halted? false\n   722\t           :blocked? false\n   723\t           :value nil)))\n   724\t\n   725\t\n   726\t(defn- vm-reset\n   727\t  \"Reset execution state, preserving the loaded AST program.\"\n   728\t  [^ASTWalkerVM vm]\n   729\t  (assoc vm\n   730\t         :control (:program vm)\n   731\t         :k nil\n   732\t         :halted? (nil? (:program vm))\n   733\t         :value nil\n   734\t         :blocked? false))\n   735\t\n   736\t\n   737\t(defn- ast-walker-run-scheduler\n   738\t  \"Thin wrapper over engine/run-loop with vm-step (slow path).\"\n   739\t  [vm]\n   740\t  (engine/run-loop vm\n   741\t                   engine/active-continuation?\n   742\t                   vm-step\n   743\t                   resume-from-run-queue\n   744\t                   ast-walker-restore))\n   745\t\n   746\t\n   747\t(defn- vm-eval\n   748\t  \"Evaluate an AST. Owns the step loop with scheduler.\n   749\t   When ast is non-nil, loads it first. When nil, resumes from current state.\"\n   750\t  [^ASTWalkerVM vm ast]\n   751\t  (let [initial-env (:env vm)\n   752\t        res (if ast\n   753\t              (-> vm\n   754\t                  (vm-load-program (vm/ast->datoms ast))\n   755\t                  (vm/run))\n   756\t              (vm/run vm))]\n   757\t    (engine/restore-initial-env initial-env res)))\n   758\t\n   759\t\n   760\t(defn- ast-walker-run-on-stream\n   761\t  [vm]\n   762\t  (engine/run-on-stream vm\n   763\t                        (:in-stream vm)\n   764\t                        vm-load-program\n   765\t                        vm-step\n   766\t                        resume-from-run-queue\n   767\t                        ast-walker-restore))\n   768\t\n   769\t\n   770\t(extend-type ASTWalkerVM\n   771\t  vm/IVM\n   772\t  (step [vm]\n   773\t    (telemetry/emit-snapshot\n   774\t      (engine/step-on-stream vm (:in-stream vm) vm-load-program vm-step)\n   775\t      :step))\n   776\t  (run [vm] (ffi/maybe-run vm ast-walker-run-on-stream))\n   777\t  (eval [vm ast] (vm-eval vm ast))\n   778\t  (reset [vm] (vm-reset vm))\n   779\t  (halted? [vm] (vm-halted? vm))\n   780\t  (blocked? [vm] (vm-blocked? vm))\n   781\t  (value [vm] (vm-value vm))\n   782\t  vm/IVMState\n   783\t  (control [vm] (:control vm))\n   784\t  (environment [vm] (:env vm))\n   785\t  (store [vm] (:store vm))\n   786\t  (continuation [vm]\n   787\t    (when-let [k-head (:k vm)]\n   788\t      (loop [k k-head\n   789\t             acc []]\n   790\t        (if (nil? k) acc (recur (:next k) (conj acc k)))))))\n   791\t\n   792\t\n   793\t(defn create-vm\n   794\t  \"Create a new ASTWalkerVM.\n   795\t\n   796\t   Options:\n   797\t     :env           initial lexical environment\n   798\t     :primitives    primitive operations map\n   799\t     :modules       module registry value (see `yin.vm.module`)\n   800\t     :make-stream   (fn [capacity] -> create outcome); no default\n   801\t     :call-in       explicit inbound request handle\n   802\t     :call-out      explicit outbound response handle\n   803\t     :call-capacity capacity for a constructed FFI pair\n   804\t     :bridge        host FFI handlers\n   805\t     :in-stream     ingress stream carrying program batches\n   806\t\n   807\t   Construction is all-or-nothing: creating the FFI pair and minting the three\n   808\t   cursors are stream operations, and any non-`ok` outcome fails here rather\n   809\t   than leaving a half-built VM.\"\n   810\t  ([] (create-vm {}))\n   811\t  ([opts]\n   812\t   (let [env (or (:env opts) {})\n   813\t         base (vm/empty-state\n   814\t                (assoc (select-keys opts\n   815\t                                    [:primitives :modules :make-stream :call-in\n   816\t                                     :call-out :call-capacity])\n   817\t                       :telemetry (:telemetry opts)\n   818\t                       :vm-model :ast-walker))\n   819\t         in-stream (:in-stream opts)\n   820\t         in-cursor (when in-stream (vm/mint-oldest in-stream :in-stream))]\n   821\t     (-> (map->ASTWalkerVM (merge base\n   822\t                                  {:bridge nil,\n   823\t                                   :in-stream in-stream,\n   824\t                                   :in-cursor in-cursor,\n   825\t                                   :ingress-gaps 0,\n   826\t                                   :program nil,\n   827\t                                   :control nil,\n   828\t                                   :env env,\n   829\t                                   :k nil,\n   830\t                                   :value nil,\n   831\t                                   :halted? true,\n   832\t                                   :blocked? false}))\n   833\t         (telemetry/install :ast-walker)\n   834\t         (ffi/attach (:bridge opts))\n   835\t         (telemetry/emit-snapshot :init)))))\n   330\t            (cond (and (contains? cardinality-many-attrs a) (vector? v))\n   331\t                  (->> v\n   332\t                       (remove nil?)\n   333\t                       (map (fn [ref] [:db/add e a ref m])))\n   334\t                  (nil? v) []\n   335\t                  :else [[:db/add e a v m]]))\n   336\t          datoms))\n   337\t\n   338\t\n   339\t(defn ast->datoms-with-root\n   340\t  \"Convert AST map into a vector of datoms. A datom is [e a v t m].\n   341\t   Returns [root-id datoms].\n   342\t\n   343\t   Entity IDs are tempids (negative integers: -16, -17, -18...) that get resolved\n   344\t   to actual entity IDs when transacted. The transactor assigns real positive IDs.\n   345\t\n   346\t   Options:\n   347\t     :t - transaction ID (default 0)\n   348\t     :m - metadata entity reference (default assert; see dao.datom/reserved)\n   349\t     :id-start - starting entity ID for tempids (default (- datom/first-user-id))\"\n   350\t  ([ast] (ast->datoms-with-root ast {}))\n   351\t  ([ast opts]\n   352\t   (let [id-counter (atom (or (:id-start opts) (- datom/first-user-id)))\n   353\t         t (or (:t opts) 0)\n   354\t         m (or (:m opts) datom/default-op)\n   355\t         gen-id #(swap! id-counter dec)\n   356\t         datoms (atom [])\n   357\t         emit! (fn [e attr val] (swap! datoms conj [e attr val t m]))\n   358\t         ;; Track pre-assigned EIDs already emitted so that a shared\n   359\t         ;; lambda-ast\n   360\t         ;; (same :eid in definition operand and call-site operator) is\n   361\t         ;; processed exactly once; every reference resolves to the same\n   362\t         ;; EID.\n   363\t         seen-eids (atom #{})]\n   364\t     (letfn\n   365\t       [(convert\n   366\t          [node]\n   367\t          (let [pre-eid (:eid node)\n   368\t                e (or pre-eid (gen-id))\n   369\t                {:keys [type tail?]} node]\n   370\t            (if (and pre-eid (contains? @seen-eids pre-eid))\n   371\t              ;; Shared reference already emitted — return EID without\n   372\t              ;; re-emitting.\n   373\t              e\n   374\t              (do\n   375\t                (when pre-eid (swap! seen-eids conj pre-eid))\n   376\t                (when tail? (emit! e :yin/tail? true))\n   377\t                (case type\n   378\t                  :literal (do (emit! e :yin/type :literal)\n   379\t                               (emit! e :yin/value (:value node)))\n   380\t                  :variable (do (emit! e :yin/type :variable)\n   381\t                                (emit! e :yin/name (:name node)))\n   382\t                  :lambda (do (emit! e :yin/type :lambda)\n   383\t                              (when (:macro? node)\n   384\t                                (emit! e :yin/macro? true)\n   385\t                                (emit! e\n   386\t                                       :yin/phase-policy\n   387\t                                       (or (:phase-policy node) :compile)))\n   388\t                              (emit! e :yin/params (:params node))\n   389\t                              (let [body-id (convert (:body node))]\n   390\t                                (emit! e :yin/body body-id)))\n   391\t                  :application (do (emit! e :yin/type :application)\n   392\t                                   (let [op-id (convert (:operator node))\n   393\t                                         operand-ids (mapv convert\n   394\t                                                           (:operands node))]\n   395\t                                     (emit! e :yin/operator op-id)\n   396\t                                     (emit! e :yin/operands operand-ids)))\n   397\t                  :dao.stream.apply/call\n   398\t                  (do (emit! e :yin/type :dao.stream.apply/call)\n   399\t                      (emit! e :yin/op (:op node))\n   400\t                      (let [operand-ids (mapv convert (:operands node))]\n   401\t                        (emit! e :yin/operands operand-ids)))\n   402\t                  :if (do (emit! e :yin/type :if)\n   403\t                          (let [test-id (convert (:test node))\n   404\t                                cons-id (convert (:consequent node))\n   405\t                                alt-id (convert (:alternate node))]\n   406\t                            (emit! e :yin/test test-id)\n   407\t                            (emit! e :yin/consequent cons-id)\n   408\t                            (emit! e :yin/alternate alt-id)))\n   409\t                  ;; VM primitives\n   410\t                  :vm/gensym (do (emit! e :yin/type :vm/gensym)\n   411\t                                 (emit! e :yin/prefix (or (:prefix node) \"id\")))\n   412\t                  :vm/store-get (do (emit! e :yin/type :vm/store-get)\n   413\t                                    (emit! e :yin/key (:key node)))\n   414\t                  :vm/store-put (do (emit! e :yin/type :vm/store-put)\n   415\t                                    (emit! e :yin/key (:key node))\n   416\t                                    (emit! e :yin/value (:val node)))\n   417\t                  ;; Stream operations\n   418\t                  :stream/make (do\n   419\t                                 (emit! e :yin/type :stream/make)\n   420\t                                 (emit! e :yin/buffer (or (:buffer node) 1024)))\n   421\t                  :stream/put (do (emit! e :yin/type :stream/put)\n   422\t                                  (let [target-id (convert (:target node))\n   423\t                                        val-id (convert (:val node))]\n   424\t                                    (emit! e :yin/target target-id)\n   425\t                                    (emit! e :yin/val-node val-id)))\n   426\t                  :stream/cursor (do (emit! e :yin/type :stream/cursor)\n   427\t                                     (let [source-id (convert (:source node))]\n   428\t                                       (emit! e :yin/source source-id)))\n   429\t                  :stream/next (do (emit! e :yin/type :stream/next)\n   430\t                                   (let [source-id (convert (:source node))]\n   431\t                                     (emit! e :yin/source source-id)))\n   432\t                  :stream/close (do (emit! e :yin/type :stream/close)\n   433\t                                    (let [source-id (convert (:source node))]\n   434\t                                      (emit! e :yin/source source-id)))\n   435\t                  ;; Continuation primitives\n   436\t                  :vm/park (emit! e :yin/type :vm/park)\n   437\t                  :vm/resume (do (emit! e :yin/type :vm/resume)\n   438\t                                 (emit! e :yin/parked-id (:parked-id node))\n   439\t                                 (let [val-id (convert (:val node))]\n   440\t                                   (emit! e :yin/val-node val-id)))\n   441\t                  :vm/current-continuation\n   442\t                  (emit! e :yin/type :vm/current-continuation)\n   443\t                  ;; Macro call site — operator is the macro lambda entity\n   444\t                  ;; ref, operands are unevaluated AST refs (not evaluated\n   445\t                  ;; runtime values)\n   446\t                  :yin/macro-expand (do (emit! e :yin/type :yin/macro-expand)\n   447\t                                        (let [op-id (convert (:operator node))\n   448\t                                              operand-ids (mapv convert\n   449\t                                                                (:operands node))]\n   450\t                                          (emit! e :yin/operator op-id)\n   451\t                                          (emit! e :yin/operands operand-ids)))\n   452\t                  ;; Default\n   453\t                  (throw (ex-info \"Unknown AST node type\"\n   454\t                                  {:type type, :node node})))\n   455\t                e))))]\n   456\t       (let [root-id (convert ast)] [root-id @datoms])))))\n   457\t\n   458\t\n   459\t(defn ast->datoms\n   460\t  \"Convert AST map into a vector of datoms. A datom is [e a v t m].\"\n   461\t  ([ast] (ast->datoms ast {}))\n   462\t  ([ast opts] (second (ast->datoms-with-root ast opts))))\n   463\t\n   464\t\n   465\t(declare index-datoms)\n   466\t\n   467\t\n   468\t(defn datoms->ast\n   469\t  \"Convert a vector of datoms [e a v t m] back into an AST map.\"\n   470\t  ([datoms]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_39","type":"agent_message","text":"# Review verdict\n\n**REQUEST CHANGES — the three benefits are only partially implemented.**\n\n`dao.stream` substantially improves explicit composition and attachment identity. `yin.vm` removes the default transport and fixes the successful FFI-call leak. But the stronger claims—true CESK serialization, strict subsystem isolation, race freedom, and bounded parked state—are not delivered.\n\nThe repository itself is unusually candid about this: [yin.vm.divergence-register.md](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.vm.divergence-register.md:239>) says the continuation is still “a linked list of frames,” while only the ast-walker evaluator is in scope.\n\n## Material findings\n\n### P1 — FFI terminal paths strand parked continuations\n\nSuccessful calls do clean up:\n\n```clojure\n(update state :parked dissoc (:call-id k))\n```\n\nThat happens only when the response continuation executes in [ast_walker.cljc](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:308>).\n\nWhen response delivery terminates because `call-out` is closed or defective, [ffi.cljc](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc:108>) marks the bridge terminal but does not remove the corresponding entry from `:parked` or `:wait-set`. [maybe-run](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc:195>) then returns that blocked VM.\n\nI reproduced:\n\n```clojure\n{:terminal :dao.stream/closed\n :parked-after-terminal 1\n :wait-after-terminal 1\n :blocked-after-terminal true}\n```\n\nLikewise, [vm-reset](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:726>) clears `:k` and flags but preserves `:parked`, `:wait-set`, and `:ready-queue`. A reset parked VM still contained one parked and one waiting entry.\n\nTherefore “unbounded accumulation in parked continuations is resolved” is true only for successful calls, not as an invariant.\n\n### P1 — WebSocket terminal ordering remains race-prone\n\n[terminal!](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc:140>) guards the close event, but other adapter entries are not terminal-gated:\n\n- `:error!` always deposits an error at [ws.cljc](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc:297>).\n- A message received after closure falls into `protocol-failure!`, which deposits another error.\n- `closed!` uses a separate dereference and update for `:resolution?` at [ws.cljc](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc:285>), so concurrent close callbacks can duplicate the pre-resolution transport error even though the terminal event itself is guarded.\n\nA direct adapter exercise produced:\n\n```clojure\n[:ws/opened :ws/closed :ws/error :ws/error]\n```\n\nSo the supposed terminal event was not terminal in practice.\n\nThere is another close/ack race: [endpoint-step](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc:467>) can observe a pending slot, then the socket callback can close its handle, after which [accept-slot!](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc:451>) unconditionally changes the phase back to `:open`. The transition needs an atomic expected-state check.\n\n### P2 — Ring-buffer attachment metadata grows without bound\n\nEvery attachment is inserted into the logical stream’s `:attachments` map at [ringbuffer.cljc](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:173>). Closing replaces its entry with:\n\n```clojure\n{:open? false :tail ...}\n```\n\nat [ringbuffer.cljc](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:137>), but nothing ever removes it.\n\nI created and closed 100 attachments on an otherwise empty stream:\n\n```clojure\n{:closed-attachment-records 100\n :retained-values 0}\n```\n\nThat is a genuine lifetime-proportional accumulation on long-lived logical streams.\n\n## Benefit assessment\n\n| Claimed benefit | Assessment | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Explicit causality and attachment identities | **Mostly delivered** | Creation and attachment are separate host-composed functions rather than protocol/global-registry behavior in [v2.cljc](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc:345>). Descriptors carry stable logical identity, and attachments receive fresh identities in [ringbuffer.cljc](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:173>) and [ws.cljc](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc:309>). |\n| Strict lifecycle/socket decoupling | **Partial** | The served logical stream and per-attachment traffic medium outlive individual sockets. However, `WsHandle` state directly contains `:socket`, and its `close!` immediately invokes that socket at [ws.cljc](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc:152>). Logical-stream lifetime is decoupled; the transport attachment is intentionally not. |\n| Pure CESK machine | **Structurally yes, operationally no** | The record exposes C/E/S/K fields, but execution uses host functions, stream handles, bridge handlers, and specialized FFI continuation types. The optimized walker also evaluates raw host functions directly. |\n| Continuations serialize as datoms | **Not implemented** | Datom conversion covers program AST nodes in [v2.cljc](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:339>). At ingress, those datoms are immediately rebuilt into AST maps in [ast_walker.cljc](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:714>). Continuations remain linked host maps, returned as a vector by [continuation](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:782>). There is no continuation-to-datoms or restore-from-datoms implementation. |\n| VM decoupled from default transport | **Delivered** | `:make-stream` is supplied by composition and has no default in [v2.cljc](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:166>). Engine code speaks only the v2 stream protocols. |\n| AST walker, FFI bridge, and stream driver strictly isolated | **Partial/mostly organizational** | `stream-driver` is cleanly generic. But `ast_walker` imports `ffi` and `apply`, constructs FFI envelopes, manipulates bridge-specific continuation types, and owns call parking in [ast_walker.cljc](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ast_walker.cljc:122>). This is file separation, not strict architectural isolation. |\n| Race conditions and parked-memory leaks closed | **Not delivered as a general property** | Happy-path cleanup and once-only FFI response delivery are real, but the terminal, reset, WebSocket, and attachment-retention cases above disprove the blanket claim. |\n\n## What is genuinely good\n\nThe v2 stream surface is a meaningful architectural improvement:\n\n- Reader, writer, closable, and descriptor capabilities are explicit protocols in [dao.stream](</Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc:140>).\n- Cursors are transport-minted and opaque rather than fabricated or incremented by the VM.\n- Expected stream outcomes are data and polling owns cadence.\n- Host-owned dispatch tables replace namespace registries.\n- The FFI bridge retains pending responses across `full` and avoids re-running handlers.\n- Successful repeated FFI calls do remove their parked entries.\n- The test suite has strong conformance, parity, retention, and two-process attachment coverage.\n\nI ran the relevant JVM/CLJC suites, excluding `yin.repl`: **144 tests, 734 assertions, all passing**. Those tests validate the intended happy paths, but they do not cover the adverse reproductions above.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nThis is a solid implementation of a narrower statement:\n\n> DaoStream v2 makes stream composition, logical identity, attachment identity, cursor ownership, and transport selection substantially more explicit; Yin VM v2 ports the ast-walker to that contract and fixes successful FFI continuation cleanup.\n\nIt does **not** yet justify “true CESK serialization” or “closing the leaks” as completed architectural properties. Those remain aspirational until runtime C/E/S/K state has a portable datom representation, bridge cancellation/terminal cleanup is exhaustive, reset has defined scheduler semantics, WebSocket terminal transitions are atomic, and closed attachment bookkeeping is bounded."}}
